WEBVTT

00:00.945 --> 00:03.414
Welcome to the Terafence
executive overview.

00:03.444 --> 00:04.959
Over the next five minutes,

00:04.989 --> 00:09.197
we’ll look at how industrial and
critical-infrastructure leaders share live

00:09.227 --> 00:12.818
operational data with the business,
AI, vendors, and regulators—

00:12.848 --> 00:15.590
while keeping plant networks
unreachable by design.

00:16.371 --> 00:21.096
Across Energy & Renewables, Oil & Gas,
Utilities, Defense & Government, Life Sciences,

00:21.126 --> 00:25.575
and Industrial Manufacturing, we’ll walk
through the risk, the hardware boundary,

00:25.605 --> 00:28.787
real deployments, and how to
validate one on your own site.

00:29.663 --> 00:33.585
Every day, operational technology—
the controllers, SCADA systems,

00:33.615 --> 00:38.667
and historians running physical plants—
sends data outward for analytics, warranties,

00:38.697 --> 00:39.528
and compliance.

00:40.288 --> 00:43.126
The risk is structural:
those links are two-way.

00:43.156 --> 00:45.579
Whoever compromises IT, a vendor account,

00:45.609 --> 00:49.983
or a VPN can follow them back into the
plant— where the impact is physical.

00:50.746 --> 00:54.424
A check valve lets water out and
shuts when anything pushes back.

00:54.454 --> 00:59.320
Terafence builds that same one-way valve
into an FPGA chip— making direction hardware,

00:59.350 --> 01:00.422
not a software rule.

01:01.205 --> 01:04.140
On the boundary, the business
still gets every reading.

01:04.170 --> 01:06.838
Firewalls stay for traffic
that must go both ways,

01:06.868 --> 01:09.210
while outbound links
lose their return path.*

01:10.080 --> 01:10.856
Why act now?

01:10.886 --> 01:15.866
Flaws are exploited in hours— often
before patches exist, and faster with AI—

01:15.896 --> 01:18.418
while 24/7 plants wait months to patch.

01:18.448 --> 01:22.045
Hardware isolation protects
OT across that entire window.

01:22.830 --> 01:25.498
Ten recent incidents prove the pattern.

01:25.528 --> 01:31.139
Eight—from Minnesota water to Polish energy
and exposed PLCs—entered from outside.

01:31.169 --> 01:36.515
Two—Jaguar Land Rover at £1.9bn and
Fairlife—began in IT yet halted production.

01:37.288 --> 01:44.796
Regulators now demand provable segmentation—
backed by NERC CIP fines, EU CRA rules,

01:44.826 --> 01:50.189
NIS2 management liability, TSA,
CMMC, FDA, and SEC mandates.

01:50.219 --> 01:55.253
CISA, NIST, and the NRC
explicitly name one-way transfer.

01:56.121 --> 01:58.876
Watch why software rules
leave boardroom doubt.

01:58.906 --> 02:01.660
A firewall runs sessions
both ways, end to end.

02:01.690 --> 02:06.377
One stolen VPN login or misconfigured rule
lets an attacker reach the controller.

02:07.080 --> 02:08.335
Switch to Terafence.

02:08.365 --> 02:12.443
Readings keep arriving in IT, but
inbound attacks stop at Side B—

02:12.473 --> 02:16.115
because no circuit in the FPGA
core carries anything back.*

02:16.871 --> 02:21.583
That answers the board’s hardest question: can
someone outside change what our plant does?

02:21.613 --> 02:25.667
Instead of hoping every rule and password
holds, the return path isn’t there.*

02:26.538 --> 02:31.992
One chassis houses three parts—with no
external servers and zero agents on PLCs.

02:32.022 --> 02:35.636
Side A acquires data, the
FPGA core moves it one way,

02:35.666 --> 02:38.333
and Side B serves a clean replica to IT.

02:39.121 --> 02:44.528
Place it at plant boundaries, between OT
zones, at remote sites, or on vendor feeds—

02:44.558 --> 02:50.093
sorting connections into four clear lanes
across standard file, web, syslog, database,

02:50.123 --> 02:51.157
and OT protocols.

02:51.913 --> 02:56.695
Every unit includes a written one-way
confirmation per serial number,

02:56.725 --> 03:01.161
an IEC 62443-4-2 SL2 assessment,
and four independent pen-tests—

03:01.191 --> 03:04.458
with clear scope on
separate VPNs and USB paths.

03:05.330 --> 03:10.162
In December’s Polish grid attack, stolen
VPN logins reached 30+ renewable sites.

03:10.192 --> 03:11.642
With a one-way boundary,

03:11.672 --> 03:15.659
telemetry still leaves while
captured routers cannot pivot inward—

03:15.689 --> 03:19.465
and during an IT breach like JLR,
one less boundary needs proof.

03:20.330 --> 03:25.228
This same architectural pattern spans six
critical sectors: Energy & Renewables;

03:25.258 --> 03:29.299
Oil & Gas; Utilities: Water,
Electric & Gas; Defense & Government;

03:29.329 --> 03:33.065
Life Sciences & Pharmaceuticals;
and Industrial Manufacturing.

03:33.830 --> 03:36.396
Within each of the six industry editions,

03:36.426 --> 03:40.558
execution reduces to three clear
lists: send routine data one way,

03:40.588 --> 03:44.535
govern true two-way flows, and
close exposed legacy paths first.

03:45.413 --> 03:48.676
Now let’s look at real-world
Terafence box case studies.

03:48.706 --> 03:50.046
In Energy & Renewables,

03:50.076 --> 03:54.971
power plants stream live stack emissions
data through a Terafence box to regulators,

03:55.001 --> 03:56.108
while in Utilities,

03:56.138 --> 04:00.038
water treatment plants feed 24/7
telemetry one way to a national SOC.

04:00.830 --> 04:03.426
In Life Sciences and
Industrial Manufacturing,

04:03.456 --> 04:08.762
a packaging-machine maker builds Terafence one-way
logic into 2,500+ pharma and food machines,

04:08.792 --> 04:13.477
while an ammonia plant streams read-only
Modbus data through a Terafence box to its

04:13.507 --> 04:16.635
equipment vendor— the same
pattern used across Oil & Gas.

04:17.413 --> 04:22.144
And in Defense & Government, military
sites isolate building controls inside OT,

04:22.174 --> 04:27.201
a public metro railway segments nine transit
systems behind dedicated Terafence boxes

04:27.231 --> 04:28.769
with a systems integrator,

04:28.799 --> 04:32.848
and a managed security provider feeds
its cloud SOC with no path back.

04:33.705 --> 04:38.354
Founded in Israel in 2015 and shipping
FPGA one-way gateways since 2017,

04:38.384 --> 04:42.905
Terafence builds industrial-grade
hardware in Taiwan for Europe, Asia,

04:42.935 --> 04:46.874
and the Americas— from DIN-rail
edge units, 1U rack gateways,

04:46.904 --> 04:51.941
and large-file models to OEM modules,
database replication, traffic mirroring,

04:51.971 --> 04:54.370
and the BSG controlled two-way gateway.

04:55.246 --> 04:59.846
You can prove it without downtime in about
two weeks: one gateway on one live outbound

04:59.876 --> 05:02.842
flow, signed off against a
written Site Acceptance Test.

05:03.663 --> 05:06.206
Three steps take you
there: assess and design,

05:06.236 --> 05:09.994
integrate beside your firewalls
with nothing installed on your PLCs,

05:10.024 --> 05:12.512
and validate with your
own reverse-path test.

05:12.542 --> 05:16.964
Thank you for listening— to discuss your
network boundaries or schedule a pilot,

05:16.994 --> 05:19.748
please reach out to us
anytime at info@terafence.us.
